          ],
          "content": "164"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.782,
            0.104,
            0.9,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "MATHEMATICS"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.139,
            0.901,
            0.158
          ],
          "content": "Also, the height of the cone = height of the top – height (radius) of the hemispherical part"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.348,
            0.17,
            0.616,
            0.216
          ],
          "content": "\\left(5 - \\frac{3.5}{2}\\right) cm  = 3.25   cm",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.231,
            0.901,
            0.282
          ],
          "content": "So, the slant height of the cone $(l)=\\sqrt{r^{2}+h^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{3.5}{2}\\right)^{2}+\\left(3.25\\right)^{2}}\\ cm=3.7\\ cm(appox.)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.295,
            0.626,
            0.339
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, CSA of cone $\\pi r l = \\left( \\frac{22}{7} \\times \\frac{3.5}{2} \\times 3.7 \\right)  cm ^2$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.345,
            0.489,
            0.364
          ],
          "content": "This gives the surface area of the top as"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.16,
            0.371,
            0.672,
            0.416
          ],
          "content": "\\left(2 \\times \\frac{22}{7} \\times \\frac{3.5}{2} \\times \\frac{3.5}{2}\\right)  cm ^2 + \\left(\\frac{22}{7} \\times \\frac{3.5}{2} \\times 3.7\\right)  cm ^2",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.426,
            0.863,
            0.469
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{22}{7} \\times \\frac{3.5}{2} (3.5 + 3.7)  cm ^2 = \\frac{11}{2} \\times (3.5 + 3.7)  cm ^2 = 39.6  cm ^2 ( approach. )",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.473,
            0.901,
            0.492
          ],
          "content": "You may note that total surface area of the top' is not the sum of the total"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.492,
            0.511,
            0.511
          ],
          "content": "surface areas of the cone and hemisphere."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.525,
            0.518,
            0.688
          ],
          "content": "Example 2 : The decorative block shown in Fig. 12.7 is made of two solids — a cube and a hemisphere. The base of the block is a cube with edge 5 cm, and the hemisphere fixed on the top has a diameter of 4.2 cm. Find the total surface area of the block. (Take $\\pi =   { \\frac { 2 2 } { 7 } }   )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.568,
            0.497,
            0.837,
            0.669
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.65,
                0.673,
                0.749,
                0.692
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 12.7"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.704,
            0.901,
            0.765
          ],
          "content": "Solution : The total surface area of the cube $6 \\times (  edge  )^{2} = 6 \\times 5 \\times 5  cm ^{2} = 150  cm ^{2}$ Note that the part of the cube where the hemisphere is attached is not included in the surface area."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.197,
            0.769,
            0.895,
            0.909
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}\\text { the surface area of the block } & = \\text { TSA of cube - base area of themisphere } \\\\& \\quad + \\text { CSA of themisphere } \\\\& = 150 - \\pi r^{2} + 2 \\pi r^{2} = (150 + \\pi r^{2}) \\mathrm{cm}^{2} \\\\& = 150 \\mathrm{cm}^{2} + \\left(\\frac{22}{7} \\times \\frac{4.2}{2} \\times \\frac{4.2}{2}\\right) \\mathrm{cm}^{2} \\\\& = (150 + 13.86) \\mathrm{cm}^{2} = 163.86 \\mathrm{cm}^{2}\\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.967,
            0.563,
            0.982
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
